  "query_preview": "..Rain and high elevation snow showers will continue \nthrough the afternoon, with a brief drying trend returning for \nSunday and Monday. The highest rainfall totals have generally been\nfrom a half inch to an inch along the Mogollon Rim, with snow\naccumulation limited to above 8000'. Once a dual trough push \neastward tonight, a ridge will briefly impact the Southwest. \nDaytime highs will rebound closer to seasonal norms, and dry \nconditions return with light winds. \n\nAnother low pressure system will swing through the state on\nTuesday/Tuesday night. The best forcing once again looks to be \nalong and southwest of the Mogollon Rim, with high confidence for\nsimilar precipitation amounts to today's system. This next system\nwill be on the slightly colder side, so here is moderate \nconfidence for light snow accumulation (up to 0.5\") down to around\n7000'. And as for locations above 8000', there is upwards of a \ncouple of inches of new snow potential. \n\nYet another brief lull in precipitation follows Wednesday into\nThursday before some degree of unsettled weather by the end of \nnext week. There is still uncertainty with the timing and \ntrajectory of the storm system later next week, so continue to \ncheck back for forecast updates. The EC ensemble maintains a \ncolder and wetter/snowier scenario, while the GFS ensemble shows \na weaker and more progressive system.",
